### Benchmarking concerns to resolve

1. **Time equivalent work.** Record global tokens per update, update count, model shape, and exact dataset subset.
2. **Define the timing boundary.** Separate preprocessing/build/allocation from steady-state training. Include optimizer updates in training-step timing.
3. **Report quality with speed.** Use the same held-out tokens and report token-weighted validation loss; perplexity is an optional derived metric.
4. **Synchronize future GPU timing.** Host timers around asynchronous launches alone will undercount work. Use completed-device timing and explicit end-to-end measurements.
5. **Measure distributed completion.** Align timed regions and report the slowest-rank elapsed time; separately account for communication.
6. **Control short-run overhead.** Initialization, logging, validation, and checkpointing can dominate a brief demonstration unless measured separately.
7. **Account for capacity-dependent clearing.** `gpt_backward` clears the entire maximum-size activation-gradient allocation, even when current `B,T` are smaller. This can distort comparisons if allocation capacities differ. `src/model.c:408`
8. **Treat documentation timing as provisional.** The reported 0.8-second figure lacks sufficient hardware and measurement detail for a benchmark baseline.

These are requirements for the forthcoming benchmark implementation; no existing GPU timing code was found to diagnose.

